 reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534986 17592186045417 true] [17592186446961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446958 13194139534986 17592186045417 true] [17592186446961 1069 "q-18081" 13194139534986 17592186045417 true] [17592186446961 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534986 17592186045417 true] [17592186446961 1071 "Find an eigenvector  of $A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -6\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2.$" 13194139534986 17592186045417 true] [17592186446961 1072 #uuid "301cb6c3-bb47-52a5-b9af-9305c0ba1695" 13194139534986 17592186045417 true] [17592186446961 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -6\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}10{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -10{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n10{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534986 17592186045417 true] [17592186446962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446957 13194139534986 17592186045417 true] [17592186446962 1069 "q-107306" 13194139534986 17592186045417 true] [17592186446962 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534986 17592186045417 true] [17592186446962 1071 "Given that $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\lambda =17$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 13\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" 13194139534986 17592186045417 true] [17592186446962 1072 #uuid "3beff671-5436-54ac-a3ba-4902dc435008" 13194139534986 17592186045417 true] [17592186446962 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 13\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}+15{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 20 & 15 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =17,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 13\\end{aligned}]-17[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 3 \\\\ 20 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-15{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 20 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534986 17592186045417 true] [17592186446963 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446959 13194139534986 17592186045417 true] [17592186446963 1069 "q-107107" 13194139534986 17592186045417 true] [17592186446963 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534986 17592186045417 true] [17592186446963 1071 "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" 13194139534986 17592186045417 true] [17592186446963 1072 #uuid "79a5f235-c3e4-525b-8b8a-0996cfbfc3bb" 13194139534986 17592186045417 true] [17592186446963 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-1}{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139534986 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0684715f-1934-531e-a1eb-44980925a4b7" 17592186446936 "answer-1123d852-cba2-552b-8381-92943f50c468" 17592186446937 "answer-1b7b31d6-cbf3-5cfa-90f0-9eb9c7ff1c04" 17592186446938 "answer-22dd36d7-fc53-51a0-aab3-39d55a21db8d" 17592186446939 "answer-24127ac8-a8dc-5354-a501-9b79d9e9cc82" 17592186446940 "answer-270f4122-201c-5e17-b091-b38dd1de9097" 17592186446941 "answer-3455a04a-3d31-5874-9183-71fedec9fd3c" 17592186446942 "answer-3cbf235c-2ab7-5f04-a833-678137504436" 17592186446943 "answer-51a64db1-d17d-5f2e-8521-b7c023059e26" 17592186446944 "answer-6e2d8f93-5ef1-55d1-b815-c1515a040b2e" 17592186446945 "answer-708ac4ba-c186-57c1-8e54-0b82ac4b423d" 17592186446946 "answer-7d7d641b-bdc6-59a8-8ec2-2c684ab7d2ad" 17592186446947 "answer-9695b750-78da-5c59-9e1c-40fb8948f0d5" 17592186446948 "answer-a2e21edc-e126-5eb1-b060-bf1813c74e47" 17592186446949 "answer-a651edc0-0c43-5e03-bb3e-0a76e26d7201" 17592186446950 "answer-aa601251-b637-5aff-9c87-3c9e773948b0" 17592186446951 "answer-b67258fc-1571-58d7-b3b9-ff66e2487d06" 17592186446952 "answer-c26f161b-d55c-5ad7-8a54-c13c5aa07aba" 17592186446953 "answer-e8e801b2-7526-5207-9e22-764415b22da1" 17592186446954 "answer-f27efeb8-a34b-557e-9386-96c0b3190fdb" 17592186446955 "answer-field-3a06f774-759e-5d50-b56f-887b8f0a8b18" 17592186446956 "answer-field-99f559f8-f0c3-5b01-8f1d-d740f6f545ba" 17592186446957 "answer-field-9ae3ba2a-4875-59fc-9d7d-d6fd0283faf6" 17592186446958 "answer-field-dd938821-c74e-5b26-90b9-683b5bbf18c0" 17592186446959 "question-111ba920-c11a-5d82-a4be-8a7616b01f46" 17592186446960 "question-301cb6c3-bb47-52a5-b9af-9305c0ba1695" 17592186446961 "question-3beff671-5436-54ac-a3ba-4902dc435008" 17592186446962 "question-79a5f235-c3e4-525b-8b8a-0996cfbfc3bb" 17592186446963} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
